00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:38.525
So before we dive into the
frontline worker, first

00:02:38.525 --> 00:02:40.650
I want to talk a little
bit about the cloud worker.

00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:42.440
This is a narrative
we kicked off,

00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:44.670
actually, this time last year.

00:02:44.670 --> 00:02:46.070
And it was a bunch
of research we

00:02:46.070 --> 00:02:49.700
did across 1,400 ID
decisionmakers and users

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:51.360
with Forrester.

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:53.450
And it was really
interesting research,

00:02:53.450 --> 00:02:58.160
that really at the end of the
day, to sum it up, it said,

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:00.680
everyone in their day-to-day
lives, their personal lives,

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:02.450
is operating in the cloud.

00:03:02.450 --> 00:03:04.920
They leverage content,
they leverage applications,

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:07.700
no matter what location
in their personal lives.

00:03:07.700 --> 00:03:09.380
Whether it's
Netflix and Spotify,

00:03:09.380 --> 00:03:11.480
whether it's getting to
work using Google Maps,

00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:13.910
with Uber and Lyft,
they're used to having

00:03:13.910 --> 00:03:16.520
information applications
at their fingertips

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:18.980
to make their day
truly efficient.

00:03:18.980 --> 00:03:23.210
And they've taken that same
expectation into Enterprises

00:03:23.210 --> 00:03:26.420
and handed that problem,
so to speak, to many of you

00:03:26.420 --> 00:03:29.060
in the room here today.

00:03:29.060 --> 00:03:31.760
But what was interesting about
the research at the time,

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:34.790
is last year, basically, it
found that about a quarter

00:03:34.790 --> 00:03:37.200
of the folks within
any organization,

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:39.500
small, medium, or large,
are cloud workers.

00:03:39.500 --> 00:03:40.190
They're ready.

00:03:40.190 --> 00:03:42.650
They're ready to
adopt the cloud.

00:03:42.650 --> 00:03:45.702
Their expectations are
there, and the excitement

00:03:45.702 --> 00:03:46.910
and the opportunity is there.

00:03:46.910 --> 00:03:50.120
And it's only grown as more
workloads move to the cloud,

00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:53.090
as the SaaS sort of
steam train continues

00:03:53.090 --> 00:03:55.460
to triple year-on-year
growth, this

00:03:55.460 --> 00:03:58.210
is becoming more
and more reality.

00:03:58.210 --> 00:04:00.590
And really it's
something that we

00:04:00.590 --> 00:04:03.680
see as a tremendous opportunity
for this frontline worker

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:05.540
segment.

00:04:05.540 --> 00:04:10.280
At the end of the day, I
think in the old world,

00:04:10.280 --> 00:04:15.560
how you worked or what your job
category was sort of determined

00:04:15.560 --> 00:04:18.110
what you needed on
a day-to-day basis.

00:04:18.110 --> 00:04:21.122
And the reality is, now, it's
not really where you work

00:04:21.122 --> 00:04:22.580
but how you work,
and what you need

00:04:22.580 --> 00:04:24.290
to do on a day-to-day basis.

00:04:24.290 --> 00:04:27.770
And anyone can fundamentally
be a cloud worker today.

00:04:27.770 --> 00:04:30.320
Whether they traditionally
are an information worker,

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:34.710
whether they're out in the
field, in the back office,

00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:36.080
they can be a cloud worker.

00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:38.697
But what's interesting is that
the segment that often gets

00:04:38.697 --> 00:04:40.280
forgotten-- and I'll
walk through this

00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:42.500
a little bit-- is
this frontline worker

00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:44.960
in terms of taking the
cloud to them so that they

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:47.900
can be more effective
and efficient in driving

00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:50.090
the organization strategy.

00:04:50.090 --> 00:04:53.390
Who are these frontline workers?

00:04:53.390 --> 00:04:55.740
Interestingly enough, it's
about 80% of the workforce.

00:04:55.740 --> 00:04:59.150
These are these deskless workers
that aren't like some of us,

00:04:59.150 --> 00:05:01.190
sort of chained to a desk.

00:05:01.190 --> 00:05:04.340
They're cashiers, they're
call center agents,

00:05:04.340 --> 00:05:07.460
they're project managers that
could be on construction sites,

00:05:07.460 --> 00:05:09.770
they're nurses, they're EMTs.

00:05:09.770 --> 00:05:12.800
They're out there in the
field working hand-in-hand,

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:15.940
selling products, supporting
products and services,

00:05:15.940 --> 00:05:19.610
and are very much the face and
the backbone of any company,

00:05:19.610 --> 00:05:23.750
or most companies that
are out there today.

00:05:23.750 --> 00:05:27.570
They directly impact the CX,
or the customer experience.

00:05:27.570 --> 00:05:31.910
And this customer experience has
huge top line and bottom line

00:05:31.910 --> 00:05:32.870
impact.

00:05:32.870 --> 00:05:37.430
As you can see here from the
Gartner study, 90% of companies

00:05:37.430 --> 00:05:40.310
compete primarily based
on customer experience.

00:05:40.310 --> 00:05:42.920
And you can see the "Harvard
Business Review" sort of backs

00:05:42.920 --> 00:05:45.860
that up with people
that have had

00:05:45.860 --> 00:05:48.770
a positive past
purchase will spend

00:05:48.770 --> 00:05:51.290
140% more on their next one.

00:05:51.290 --> 00:05:53.700
And I think we can
all relate to that.

00:05:53.700 --> 00:05:57.230
I, way, way, way back when,
when I was in the restaurant

00:05:57.230 --> 00:06:00.050
business and was a
frontline bartender, waiter,

00:06:00.050 --> 00:06:02.720
what have you, or worked
in the hotel business,

00:06:02.720 --> 00:06:05.693
I had managers that always
said if a customer has

00:06:05.693 --> 00:06:07.110
a bad experience,
they're properly

00:06:07.110 --> 00:06:08.605
going to tell 5
to 10 more people

00:06:08.605 --> 00:06:10.230
than they would if
they had a good one.

00:06:10.230 --> 00:06:13.340
So this has both a positive
and negative impact.

00:06:13.340 --> 00:06:16.760
And executives and companies
are seeing this and measuring

00:06:16.760 --> 00:06:19.480
the performance of this.

00:06:19.480 --> 00:06:23.320
However, the same token,
these same leaders

00:06:23.320 --> 00:06:27.010
feel like the
investment isn't there

00:06:27.010 --> 00:06:28.270
in these frontline workers--

00:06:28.270 --> 00:06:30.340
not giving them
the tools they need

00:06:30.340 --> 00:06:34.060
to really elevate and meet
the needs of this customer

00:06:34.060 --> 00:06:34.960
experience.

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:37.540
And you can see it here in
terms of the frontline worker

00:06:37.540 --> 00:06:40.730
managers themselves and
those actual workers.

00:06:40.730 --> 00:06:45.670
75% of them feel like they don't
have consistent communication

00:06:45.670 --> 00:06:48.670
to really carry the message,
both from a compliance

00:06:48.670 --> 00:06:51.040
standpoint and a
strategy standpoint,

00:06:51.040 --> 00:06:53.780
to their customers to
do their day-to-day job.

00:06:53.780 --> 00:06:57.820
So it's a huge missed
opportunity in many ways.

00:06:57.820 --> 00:06:59.590
And again, it's,
interestingly enough,

00:06:59.590 --> 00:07:03.070
validated by both the leaders
within these organizations

00:07:03.070 --> 00:07:08.837
as well as the frontline worker
managers and their users.

00:07:08.837 --> 00:07:10.420
We actually did some
surveying ourself

00:07:10.420 --> 00:07:12.240
to sort of test
this out as well,

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:15.070
and asked both information
workers and frontline

00:07:15.070 --> 00:07:17.380
workers what kind
of effect technology

00:07:17.380 --> 00:07:19.900
has had in terms of
a positive effect

00:07:19.900 --> 00:07:21.490
over the last past years--

00:07:21.490 --> 00:07:23.590
past five years, sorry.

00:07:23.590 --> 00:07:26.490
Information workers, 70% felt
like it had a positive effect.

00:07:26.490 --> 00:07:29.410
Frontline workers,
one out of every two

00:07:29.410 --> 00:07:30.945
said it had a positive impact.

00:07:30.945 --> 00:07:32.320
So think about
that for a minute.

00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:35.830
There has been a lot of
positive technical changes

00:07:35.830 --> 00:07:38.650
in the marketplace, but still
only one out of every two

00:07:38.650 --> 00:07:42.010
frontline workers actually feel
like it helps their experience

00:07:42.010 --> 00:07:43.960
to do their job better, right?

00:07:43.960 --> 00:07:46.847
So huge missed opportunity here.

00:07:46.847 --> 00:07:47.930
But what does it all mean?

00:07:47.930 --> 00:07:48.888
What does it result in?

00:07:48.888 --> 00:07:51.040
Why should you care?

00:07:51.040 --> 00:07:54.820
At the end of the day, is
that if you have disengagement

00:07:54.820 --> 00:07:57.550
here-- and you can see
here, if you have pessimism

00:07:57.550 --> 00:07:59.740
within this frontline
worker base,

00:07:59.740 --> 00:08:01.660
and you can see the
statistics here.

00:08:01.660 --> 00:08:05.590
Back in 2016, there
is a 66% turnover rate

00:08:05.590 --> 00:08:08.290
within these frontline workers.

00:08:08.290 --> 00:08:12.730
And underneath this is, if
you take a semi-average,

00:08:12.730 --> 00:08:15.940
hourly employee-- they
make $30,000 a year--

00:08:15.940 --> 00:08:20.650
research shows that about
20% to 25% of that salary

00:08:20.650 --> 00:08:22.468
is needed to hire
the next employee.

00:08:22.468 --> 00:08:24.760
So you can see how you get
to this hundreds of billions

00:08:24.760 --> 00:08:28.330
of dollars of lost productivity
and revenue to companies

00:08:28.330 --> 00:08:29.500
every year.

00:08:29.500 --> 00:08:33.919
It's pretty staggering
and amazing.

00:08:33.919 --> 00:08:34.900
It's not all bad news.

00:08:34.900 --> 00:08:36.289
There is some good news here.

00:08:36.289 --> 00:08:38.419
And the good news here
is we really think,

00:08:38.419 --> 00:08:40.010
and we have some proof--

00:08:40.010 --> 00:08:42.020
both from outside
research as well as

00:08:42.020 --> 00:08:44.690
experiences with ourselves
and customers like Stitch

00:08:44.690 --> 00:08:47.570
Fix-- that the cloud can
help solve this, right?

00:08:47.570 --> 00:08:51.852
It can really help elevate
these frontline workers in terms

00:08:51.852 --> 00:08:53.060
of their employee experience.

00:08:53.060 --> 00:08:54.290
They're ready.

00:08:54.290 --> 00:08:55.868
They, like all of us--

00:08:55.868 --> 00:08:57.410
and some of them
may be in the room--

00:08:57.410 --> 00:09:00.780
are using the cloud in their
day-to-day personal lives.

00:09:00.780 --> 00:09:03.590
So their usage and their
expectation level is there.

00:09:03.590 --> 00:09:05.360
And if they are more
engaged, and they

00:09:05.360 --> 00:09:07.070
are having a more
positive experience,

00:09:07.070 --> 00:09:09.470
that's going to pass on to
the customer experience,

00:09:09.470 --> 00:09:11.960
and have that positive impact.

00:09:11.960 --> 00:09:13.670
On top of that, the
cloud can really

00:09:13.670 --> 00:09:16.282
deliver a higher
level of security.

00:09:16.282 --> 00:09:17.990
And we've seen that
in Chrome Enterprise,

00:09:17.990 --> 00:09:19.850
and we'll talk a
little bit about that.

00:09:19.850 --> 00:09:22.360
And ideally-- and we've
seen this as well--

00:09:22.360 --> 00:09:25.220
is going to deliver you a more
productive solution at a lower

00:09:25.220 --> 00:09:25.980
cost.

00:09:25.980 --> 00:09:28.340
So all of these elements are
really the positive light

00:09:28.340 --> 00:09:29.870
that we want to talk a
little bit about in terms

00:09:29.870 --> 00:09:32.530
of the opportunity that you
have with your frontline worker

00:09:32.530 --> 00:09:34.353
segment.

00:09:34.353 --> 00:09:36.770
For Chrome Enterprise, for
those of you that are not aware

00:09:36.770 --> 00:09:40.550
of it, this really encompasses
Chrome browser, Chrome OS,

00:09:40.550 --> 00:09:43.640
and Chromebooks, as well as
the management capabilities

00:09:43.640 --> 00:09:45.950
that come with this offering.

00:09:45.950 --> 00:09:51.860
You can see here on the
left side of the slide,

00:09:51.860 --> 00:09:53.660
you have what it brings
to the business--

00:09:53.660 --> 00:09:57.020
so folks that are here in the
room managing end points or end

00:09:57.020 --> 00:09:57.890
users--

00:09:57.890 --> 00:09:59.690
as well as on the
right, what benefits

00:09:59.690 --> 00:10:00.830
it can bring to the users.

00:10:00.830 --> 00:10:03.980
And I'll walk through
each aspect of this

00:10:03.980 --> 00:10:07.130
as we go through
these slides here.

00:10:07.130 --> 00:10:08.570
First of all, security.

00:10:08.570 --> 00:10:11.600
I think those of you that have
been on of the Chrome journey

00:10:11.600 --> 00:10:15.320
hopefully know at this point
that security is really

00:10:15.320 --> 00:10:18.800
paramount, and probably
the first key pillar

00:10:18.800 --> 00:10:22.040
to any feature, or
device, or functionality

00:10:22.040 --> 00:10:23.540
that we roll out with Chrome.

00:10:23.540 --> 00:10:25.430
And it really is
the highest bar,

00:10:25.430 --> 00:10:29.070
both from browser, to OS, to any
Chrome device that's out there.

00:10:29.070 --> 00:10:31.548
And we have multi
layers of security.

00:10:31.548 --> 00:10:33.590
And we could definitely
take you through reviews.

00:10:33.590 --> 00:10:35.510
But I think what we
can say confidently

00:10:35.510 --> 00:10:37.640
is that most folks
that go through this

00:10:37.640 --> 00:10:39.770
and look at it from
a security standpoint

00:10:39.770 --> 00:10:41.510
are pretty well pleased.

00:10:41.510 --> 00:10:43.460
We've put a lot
of work into sort

00:10:43.460 --> 00:10:47.300
of isolating certain encrypted
profiles and management

00:10:47.300 --> 00:10:48.770
of applications.

00:10:48.770 --> 00:10:52.900
And the beauty of Chrome is that
it does update every six weeks.

00:10:52.900 --> 00:10:56.480
And most all those updates
have security capabilities

00:10:56.480 --> 00:10:57.350
that are added.

00:10:57.350 --> 00:10:59.870
And those are things that
you can either take advantage

00:10:59.870 --> 00:11:04.180
of or manage and roll those
out as time is needed, right?

00:11:04.180 --> 00:11:07.490
But security is definitely a
really high bar, and a true

00:11:07.490 --> 00:11:11.060
differentiator when it
comes to Chrome Enterprise.

00:11:11.060 --> 00:11:12.620
From a licensing
standpoint, what you

00:11:12.620 --> 00:11:15.320
get with the license
is a number of things.

00:11:15.320 --> 00:11:17.930
But just to point out a
couple here is you get,

00:11:17.930 --> 00:11:22.250
really, policy management across
your users and your end points.

00:11:22.250 --> 00:11:27.057
So 200+ of them think of
it as if you have a segment

00:11:27.057 --> 00:11:29.390
of users-- so a set of frontline
workers that are mobile

00:11:29.390 --> 00:11:31.400
workers-- and you
have an application,

00:11:31.400 --> 00:11:34.070
or a set of applications that
you want them to have access

00:11:34.070 --> 00:11:36.630
to, you can push
those to those users.

00:11:36.630 --> 00:11:38.520
You can also blacklist
certain applications

00:11:38.520 --> 00:11:40.060
so they don't have
access to those.

00:11:40.060 --> 00:11:43.730
So it gives you kind of a full
breadth of user management

00:11:43.730 --> 00:11:44.870
in that regard.

00:11:44.870 --> 00:11:46.530
You also have device management.

00:11:46.530 --> 00:11:49.760
So that if a device
disappears or gets

00:11:49.760 --> 00:11:53.030
lost you can essentially
disable that device

00:11:53.030 --> 00:11:57.690
so it is non-operable and causes
no harm to your environment.

00:11:57.690 --> 00:11:59.210
So it gives you
the full breadth.

00:11:59.210 --> 00:12:01.640
And we're constantly
updating those policies.

00:12:01.640 --> 00:12:04.670
We've also-- the product team's
done a phenomenal job working

00:12:04.670 --> 00:12:07.580
with a set of our partners
that are in this EMM space

00:12:07.580 --> 00:12:10.280
to open up our APIs so
that you can leverage,

00:12:10.280 --> 00:12:13.200
if you've made investment
in any of these providers,

00:12:13.200 --> 00:12:15.170
you can have that
single pane of glass

00:12:15.170 --> 00:12:17.510
and still manage your
devices, like you may manage

00:12:17.510 --> 00:12:20.600
a mobile device or any other
device you have in your fleet.

00:12:20.600 --> 00:12:23.990
So we're trying to really help
bridge the gap between what

00:12:23.990 --> 00:12:28.160
you've purchased in the past,
as well as sort of enabling

00:12:28.160 --> 00:12:29.090
that for the future.

00:12:29.090 --> 00:12:32.960
And then obviously you get a lot
of the common Enterprise 24/7

00:12:32.960 --> 00:12:37.430
support that you would get
with any Enterprise offering.

00:12:37.430 --> 00:12:40.820
Savings is a big piece of
this, and yeah, a couple of

00:12:40.820 --> 00:12:42.740
slides here to
talk through that.

00:12:42.740 --> 00:12:44.090
Google is a great example.

00:12:44.090 --> 00:12:47.570
We still are a company that
allows ultimate flexibility

00:12:47.570 --> 00:12:51.020
in terms of what types of
operating system and devices

00:12:51.020 --> 00:12:52.470
you can utilize.

00:12:52.470 --> 00:12:55.940
We're seeing, obviously,
a high growth, and Chrome

00:12:55.940 --> 00:12:58.305
being a huge segment of that.

00:12:58.305 --> 00:13:00.180
But you can see some of
the comparables here.

00:13:00.180 --> 00:13:02.030
And I think it's
really something

00:13:02.030 --> 00:13:05.090
that a lot of times
customers don't really

00:13:05.090 --> 00:13:06.970
fathom until they've
experienced it

00:13:06.970 --> 00:13:10.010
is that, one, the setup of
the device can be far simpler.

00:13:10.010 --> 00:13:13.250
So you can see here it's
approximately 33 minutes

00:13:13.250 --> 00:13:14.750
to set up a Chrome
device and close

00:13:14.750 --> 00:13:16.670
to 2 and 1/2 hours for
a comparable device

00:13:16.670 --> 00:13:18.330
with the other platforms.

00:13:18.330 --> 00:13:20.630
And this is coming from
our IT support teams

00:13:20.630 --> 00:13:23.663
at Google that are supporting
tens of thousands of these.

00:13:23.663 --> 00:13:25.580
Once you have a device
out there in the field,

00:13:25.580 --> 00:13:27.247
then there's the
support of that device.

00:13:27.247 --> 00:13:30.728
And we measure that
ticket per 1,000 tickets

00:13:30.728 --> 00:13:31.520
that are out there.

00:13:31.520 --> 00:13:34.190
And it's about 19
tickets per 1,000

00:13:34.190 --> 00:13:36.230
are Chrome tickets,
and almost 100

00:13:36.230 --> 00:13:37.650
for a comparable windows device.

00:13:37.650 --> 00:13:40.580
So we are a great
testament of the fact

00:13:40.580 --> 00:13:44.090
that both the setup and the
support of these devices

00:13:44.090 --> 00:13:48.530
is far lower than what we're
finding in our environment.

00:13:48.530 --> 00:13:50.720
And we continue to
sort of pull this,

00:13:50.720 --> 00:13:52.730
and happy to share some
of these statistics

00:13:52.730 --> 00:13:56.210
with you if it'll help
in your engagements.

00:13:56.210 --> 00:13:59.630
This multi-user experience,
again, is something that--

00:13:59.630 --> 00:14:04.220
sometimes candidly, we struggle
to explain to companies

00:14:04.220 --> 00:14:05.580
and the uniqueness of it.

00:14:05.580 --> 00:14:08.407
And oftentimes when they
start to deploy Chrome,

00:14:08.407 --> 00:14:09.740
sort of the light bulb goes off.

00:14:09.740 --> 00:14:15.470
But in essence, you can have as
many encrypted cloud profiles

00:14:15.470 --> 00:14:16.740
running on these devices.

00:14:16.740 --> 00:14:19.580
So much like-- if you're
familiar with education,

00:14:19.580 --> 00:14:23.600
and Chromebooks, and 60 plus
percent market share in the US

00:14:23.600 --> 00:14:27.140
here, alone, is a big part
of that value proposition was

00:14:27.140 --> 00:14:29.180
kids could jump
into a classroom,

00:14:29.180 --> 00:14:32.240
pull a Chromebook off the shelf,
and use that in a secure way.

00:14:32.240 --> 00:14:34.610
We've taken that to
the Enterprise as well,

00:14:34.610 --> 00:14:37.430
so that amongst your frontline
workers, many of them

00:14:37.430 --> 00:14:41.060
may have multiple shifts
throughout the course of a day.

00:14:41.060 --> 00:14:43.580
And so you can have
those same set of devices

00:14:43.580 --> 00:14:47.750
be used by a large magnitude of
frontline workers, by the way,

00:14:47.750 --> 00:14:49.410
for a bunch of
different use cases.

00:14:49.410 --> 00:14:51.590
So it securely
knows who you are,

00:14:51.590 --> 00:14:54.810
what applications
you have access to.

00:14:54.810 --> 00:14:59.150
You then can set it so that it
wipes automatically on log out,

00:14:59.150 --> 00:15:03.210
or you can set it to time
out and wipe those devices.

00:15:03.210 --> 00:15:05.270
So all the security
components are there.

00:15:05.270 --> 00:15:06.950
The next front line
worker comes in can

00:15:06.950 --> 00:15:09.440
pull that Chromebook off the
shelf and do what they need.

00:15:09.440 --> 00:15:12.320
This is a piece that,
when I talk to any CIOs

00:15:12.320 --> 00:15:15.860
right on down to the IT folks
that are managing devices,

00:15:15.860 --> 00:15:17.390
really believe this
is unparalleled,

00:15:17.390 --> 00:15:20.500
like the slide says, in terms
of a differentiated capability.

00:15:20.500 --> 00:15:22.610
And it's something that
allows you, quite frankly,

00:15:22.610 --> 00:15:25.220
to deploy less devices
out in the field,

00:15:25.220 --> 00:15:28.340
but serve more users.

00:15:28.340 --> 00:15:33.410
This again sort of speaks
a bit to the savings here.

00:15:33.410 --> 00:15:37.715
Dave over Schnucks, a
grocery chain in the Midwest,

00:15:37.715 --> 00:15:38.840
has been using Chromebooks.

00:15:38.840 --> 00:15:41.230
And you can see
here, quite openly,

00:15:41.230 --> 00:15:45.432
is saving $1,250 per
year on device costs.

00:15:45.432 --> 00:15:47.390
And you could see on the
left-hand side, again,

00:15:47.390 --> 00:15:49.660
it starts a bit with the
hardware and software

00:15:49.660 --> 00:15:51.320
and initial investment.

00:15:51.320 --> 00:15:53.720
There's a bit here in terms
of employee productivity.

00:15:53.720 --> 00:15:58.250
So being able to be productive
on a day-to-day basis

00:15:58.250 --> 00:15:59.390
is a component of it.

00:15:59.390 --> 00:16:01.880
But I think again,
the 75% improvement

00:16:01.880 --> 00:16:06.980
in terms of IT productivity and
savings on what IT is used to

00:16:06.980 --> 00:16:08.450
is really a big proponent.

00:16:08.450 --> 00:16:12.170
And I often say when I chat with
CXOs those that are out there,

00:16:12.170 --> 00:16:15.030
they always have more
projects than people.

00:16:15.030 --> 00:16:16.982
So if you can save
them time on this,

00:16:16.982 --> 00:16:18.440
there's plenty of
projects for them

00:16:18.440 --> 00:16:21.860
to pick up outside of managing
devices and end points.

00:16:21.860 --> 00:16:24.920
And Chrome can give
you that capability.

00:16:24.920 --> 00:16:28.160
I think, from a user
standpoint, a lot

00:16:28.160 --> 00:16:30.540
of these things we've been
talking about for years,

00:16:30.540 --> 00:16:32.030
but I think are good reminders.

00:16:32.030 --> 00:16:35.410
The fact that a Chromebook can
boot up in about six seconds

00:16:35.410 --> 00:16:37.090
is still pretty amazing.

00:16:37.090 --> 00:16:38.840
A lot of us has been
living in that world,

00:16:38.840 --> 00:16:41.810
so we forget the old world
of turn the device on, grab

00:16:41.810 --> 00:16:43.790
a cup of coffee, come
back and be productive.

00:16:43.790 --> 00:16:48.320
This again is something that
is definitely a differentiator

00:16:48.320 --> 00:16:52.670
and continues to be a big
piece of the value proposition.

00:16:52.670 --> 00:16:55.550
The cloud based profiles is
something I mentioned before.

00:16:55.550 --> 00:16:56.540
The saved ID time.

00:16:56.540 --> 00:16:58.790
And then basically,
quick access to what

00:16:58.790 --> 00:17:00.460
you need to do on
a day-to-day basis.

00:17:00.460 --> 00:17:04.010
And we're always trying to
incorporate things around ML

00:17:04.010 --> 00:17:05.750
in the background,
with our partners

00:17:05.750 --> 00:17:07.700
and with our
employees, to help make

00:17:07.700 --> 00:17:10.595
it a more productive
experience on a device.

00:17:10.595 --> 00:17:13.220
It's something that's unique to
allow them to do what they need

00:17:13.220 --> 00:17:16.280
to do on a day-to-day basis.

00:17:16.280 --> 00:17:19.188
In terms of form factors, we've
made a lot of progress here.

00:17:19.188 --> 00:17:21.230
I think when I started
about six, seven years ago

00:17:21.230 --> 00:17:24.710
we had one OEM and one
device that we were

00:17:24.710 --> 00:17:26.630
deploying across the globe.

00:17:26.630 --> 00:17:32.180
Now, you can see that every
major OEM has a set of devices

00:17:32.180 --> 00:17:33.380
in the marketplace.

00:17:33.380 --> 00:17:34.880
I think what's
pretty exciting, too,

00:17:34.880 --> 00:17:37.890
is that it runs the gamut
in terms of form factor--

00:17:37.890 --> 00:17:41.890
from tablet, to convertible,
to desktop, to clam shell.

00:17:41.890 --> 00:17:44.070
And really, what's
great about it

00:17:44.070 --> 00:17:46.950
when you think about your
segments within your workforce,

00:17:46.950 --> 00:17:49.260
is everything from
lower end, to medium,

00:17:49.260 --> 00:17:51.130
to really premium devices.

00:17:51.130 --> 00:17:53.610
And I think we're really
excited for the future

00:17:53.610 --> 00:17:55.120
here, because I
think we're going

00:17:55.120 --> 00:17:57.600
to continue to build out
that portfolio with our OEM

00:17:57.600 --> 00:17:58.530
partners.

00:17:58.530 --> 00:18:00.300
So it gives you the
breadth and depth

00:18:00.300 --> 00:18:02.970
you need to meet the needs
of not just your front line

00:18:02.970 --> 00:18:06.030
workers, but in the future,
more and more your information

00:18:06.030 --> 00:18:09.060
workers as well.

00:18:09.060 --> 00:18:11.610
G Suite is something
here that we definitely

00:18:11.610 --> 00:18:12.570
wanted to mention.

00:18:12.570 --> 00:18:15.570
I think many of our
Chrome customers

00:18:15.570 --> 00:18:19.600
started as G Suite customers,
which makes a lot of sense.

00:18:19.600 --> 00:18:21.690
They've evolved and
are using the cloud

00:18:21.690 --> 00:18:24.420
for their main
collaboration applications

00:18:24.420 --> 00:18:26.820
and have really bought
in and invested there.

00:18:26.820 --> 00:18:29.790
And so Chrome is a logical fit.

00:18:29.790 --> 00:18:31.710
And we continue to
work across our teams

00:18:31.710 --> 00:18:35.520
to figure out ways to bring
a unique value in this.

00:18:35.520 --> 00:18:38.100
At the same time, we
have many, many customers

00:18:38.100 --> 00:18:40.853
that deploy Chrome
browser, then Chrome OS,

00:18:40.853 --> 00:18:42.270
and then start to
think about what

00:18:42.270 --> 00:18:44.270
applications they could
be running in the cloud.

00:18:44.270 --> 00:18:46.300
So it does go both ways.

00:18:46.300 --> 00:18:47.820
And I think what
we really try to do

00:18:47.820 --> 00:18:49.745
is bring the best
of both worlds here.

00:18:49.745 --> 00:18:51.120
And a lot of times
it's customers

00:18:51.120 --> 00:18:52.870
who are thinking about this.

00:18:52.870 --> 00:18:54.390
But I think what
we definitely have

00:18:54.390 --> 00:18:58.020
found is those of you that
have invested in G Suite

00:18:58.020 --> 00:19:00.240
and have not deployed
Chromebooks, what it will do

00:19:00.240 --> 00:19:02.500
is you will get more
out of your investment,

00:19:02.500 --> 00:19:05.790
because you're really pushing
people to be cloud-enabled,

00:19:05.790 --> 00:19:08.070
and it'll allow you to
get better value out

00:19:08.070 --> 00:19:11.610
of those investments
you've made in the cloud.

00:19:11.610 --> 00:19:14.570
A couple of examples before
I turn things over to Ravi,

00:19:14.570 --> 00:19:17.460
that I think bring
this a bit to life.

00:19:17.460 --> 00:19:21.480
Middlesex Hospital Health Care
is one of our target verticals,

00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:24.165
has deployed Chromebooks
in a number of use cases.

00:19:24.165 --> 00:19:26.040
I won't go through all
elements of the slide,

00:19:26.040 --> 00:19:28.020
but, in essence,
they're deploying it

00:19:28.020 --> 00:19:29.560
for many use cases.

00:19:29.560 --> 00:19:33.570
As an example, nurses that
need ongoing compliance and HR

00:19:33.570 --> 00:19:37.260
training that are coming in
multiple times of the day

00:19:37.260 --> 00:19:39.330
and multiple
shifts, can leverage

00:19:39.330 --> 00:19:42.750
Chromebooks either one-to-one
or in a shared environment

00:19:42.750 --> 00:19:43.860
to get that training.

00:19:43.860 --> 00:19:46.020
They're using it for EMTs.

00:19:46.020 --> 00:19:49.380
So think about use cases
where every second matters.

00:19:49.380 --> 00:19:52.240
Same type of thing for EMTs
to get the training they need,

00:19:52.240 --> 00:19:54.240
but also to support
their day-to-day business

00:19:54.240 --> 00:19:56.115
when they're out there
working with patients.

00:19:56.115 --> 00:19:57.660
And at the end of
the day, again, it

00:19:57.660 --> 00:19:59.320
goes back to that
customer experience.

00:19:59.320 --> 00:20:03.340
It's all about how can the
patient experience be improved?

00:20:03.340 --> 00:20:06.930
And if Chrome devices can help
from a productivity standpoint,

00:20:06.930 --> 00:20:10.110
so they can spend more time with
their patients, it's a win-win.

00:20:10.110 --> 00:20:11.790
And they're seeing
that in spades.

00:20:11.790 --> 00:20:17.270
And are always looking for more
use cases to add Chrome to.

00:20:17.270 --> 00:20:20.970
Ervia is a utility
company in Ireland.

00:20:20.970 --> 00:20:22.980
And so you can imagine
the use cases here

00:20:22.980 --> 00:20:26.370
in terms of highly distributed
workforce across the country

00:20:26.370 --> 00:20:28.800
supporting a bunch of
different frontline workers

00:20:28.800 --> 00:20:30.930
that are out in the field.

00:20:30.930 --> 00:20:35.347
And Chrome has enabled them,
both from a grab-and-go, which

00:20:35.347 --> 00:20:37.680
hopefully if you haven't seen
it here at the conference,

00:20:37.680 --> 00:20:41.160
there's a series of
grab-and-go booths, where

00:20:41.160 --> 00:20:44.070
you can essentially think
of it as loaner devices.

00:20:44.070 --> 00:20:46.128
They're leveraging
these for folks

00:20:46.128 --> 00:20:47.670
that may have lost
the device or just

00:20:47.670 --> 00:20:50.880
any device for a couple of hours
to do their time-keeping, or HR

00:20:50.880 --> 00:20:53.190
capabilities, or any
other applications they

00:20:53.190 --> 00:20:58.150
need to access, as well as what
they need out in the field.

00:20:58.150 --> 00:21:02.310
So again, Chrome is a great
enabler here for applications

00:21:02.310 --> 00:21:04.330
that are not cloud-enabled.

00:21:04.330 --> 00:21:05.730
They're also leveraging VDI.

00:21:05.730 --> 00:21:08.580
So we've made a lot of
efforts over the years

00:21:08.580 --> 00:21:14.340
to make sure, if you don't have
those applications in the cloud

00:21:14.340 --> 00:21:16.470
yet, or maybe they're
not Android applications

00:21:16.470 --> 00:21:18.660
because those are compatible
with Chrome as well,

00:21:18.660 --> 00:21:24.150
VDI is a bridge that'll
help you get there as well.

00:21:24.150 --> 00:21:25.320
A couple more.

00:21:25.320 --> 00:21:26.880
Mercado Libre.

00:21:26.880 --> 00:21:30.767
Latin America company in Mexico,
actually, that's flourishing;

00:21:30.767 --> 00:21:32.850
leveraging Chromebooks for
contact centers, right?

00:21:32.850 --> 00:21:35.910
So I think we really
think of contact centers

00:21:35.910 --> 00:21:38.335
as a great frontline
worker segment.

00:21:38.335 --> 00:21:39.960
We're seeing more
and more like Mercado

00:21:39.960 --> 00:21:44.700
where this is a
outsourced function, one

00:21:44.700 --> 00:21:47.670
that has a lot of
remote requirements,

00:21:47.670 --> 00:21:51.000
and oftentimes IT does
not have the bandwidth

00:21:51.000 --> 00:21:54.810
to support these
growing customer service

00:21:54.810 --> 00:21:56.290
and contact agents.

00:21:56.290 --> 00:21:59.370
And I think Mercado is a
great case of where they now

00:21:59.370 --> 00:22:02.850
have contact center agents that,
again, can be up and running

00:22:02.850 --> 00:22:05.730
within 6 to 10 seconds,
can really meet

00:22:05.730 --> 00:22:07.210
the needs of their customer.

00:22:07.210 --> 00:22:10.590
So in essence, they're happier,
so their customers are happier.

00:22:10.590 --> 00:22:12.440
So again, a great use case.

00:22:12.440 --> 00:22:14.700
It is one where we have
a number of companies

00:22:14.700 --> 00:22:17.370
that are utilizing
this, and it's sort of

00:22:17.370 --> 00:22:18.475
come to our attention.

00:22:18.475 --> 00:22:20.600
Many of them were doing it
without us even knowing.

00:22:20.600 --> 00:22:22.540
They just they just
stumbled on the use case.

00:22:22.540 --> 00:22:25.950
And I think as more and more
contact center applications

00:22:25.950 --> 00:22:29.040
move to the cloud, this
is an outstanding use case

00:22:29.040 --> 00:22:32.272
to start with in
any organization.

00:22:32.272 --> 00:22:33.730
And then the last
one before I turn

00:22:33.730 --> 00:22:35.190
things over is Charles Schwab.

00:22:35.190 --> 00:22:38.140
They've been a customer
a couple of years now.

00:22:38.140 --> 00:22:40.220
It is a financial services
company, of course,

00:22:40.220 --> 00:22:44.980
but think of it as very much
a retail frontline worker use

00:22:44.980 --> 00:22:46.870
case, in that they have
deployed Chromebooks

00:22:46.870 --> 00:22:48.640
across all their branches.

00:22:48.640 --> 00:22:52.330
And really, like many of these
wealth management companies,

00:22:52.330 --> 00:22:55.360
their goal is to really
empower their customers

00:22:55.360 --> 00:22:59.290
to utilize the investment they
made in online applications,

00:22:59.290 --> 00:23:01.180
both for financial
planning, portfolio

00:23:01.180 --> 00:23:02.410
planning, and the like.

00:23:02.410 --> 00:23:04.480
And so they have
deployed Chrome devices

00:23:04.480 --> 00:23:06.670
so that anyone who
comes into the branch

00:23:06.670 --> 00:23:09.670
can either work with a
representative on their wealth

00:23:09.670 --> 00:23:11.800
planning, but ideally
that representative

00:23:11.800 --> 00:23:14.260
turns a Chromebook around
and says, help yourself.

00:23:14.260 --> 00:23:15.910
Here, this is where
you log in, this

00:23:15.910 --> 00:23:18.850
is how you can utilize that,
so they can become much more

00:23:18.850 --> 00:23:19.600
self-sufficient.

00:23:19.600 --> 00:23:22.300
So hopefully you can
think of many use cases

00:23:22.300 --> 00:23:25.750
within your organization that
are similar to that, which

00:23:25.750 --> 00:23:28.990
have that element of helping
from a customer satisfaction

00:23:28.990 --> 00:23:31.360
standpoint, but also helping
them serve themselves

00:23:31.360 --> 00:23:33.350
by utilizing their
device as well.

00:23:33.350 --> 00:23:35.470
So those are a few use cases.

00:23:35.470 --> 00:23:38.110
Again, I just touched on sort
of the high-level pieces.

00:23:38.110 --> 00:23:40.930
Obviously, we'll take some
questions here at the end,

00:23:40.930 --> 00:23:41.980
but I think--

00:23:41.980 --> 00:23:44.225
I'm really excited to
bring Ravi up here who

00:23:44.225 --> 00:23:45.850
can speak a little
bit in depth on what

00:23:45.850 --> 00:23:47.017
they're doing at Stitch Fix.

00:23:47.017 --> 00:23:49.476
So if you could please
give him a warm welcome.

